    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Oral glutathione has modest but repeatedly measured evidence for evening skin tone
        and fading recent pigmentation at 250mg to 500mg daily — and none for changing your natural
        skin colour.</strong> It works by inhibiting tyrosinase, the enzyme that starts melanin
        production. Expect 8 to 12 weeks, expect the effect to fade once you stop, and expect
        nothing at all from a short course.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Studied dose:</strong> 250mg once daily, 250mg twice daily, or 500mg once daily. A 2025 systematic review in the <em>International Journal of Dermatology</em> found five randomised trials at those doses reduced the melanin index against placebo.</li>
        <li><strong>The caveat that belongs next to it:</strong> the largest placebo-controlled trial, in 83 Indonesian adults over 12 weeks, saw the glutathione group improve more than placebo but <em>miss statistical significance</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Timeline:</strong> 4 weeks at the earliest; 8–12 weeks for anything you would notice in a photograph.</li>
        <li><strong>Vitamin C is not optional.</strong> It regenerates glutathione back to its active form, which is why the two are almost always paired.</li>
        <li><strong>Effects are reversible.</strong> Pigmentation returns after you stop, particularly without daily sun protection.</li>
        <li><strong>Skip the IV route.</strong> India's drug regulator told clinics in May 2026 that no cosmetic may be injected — glutathione "glow drips" included.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

