      <h2 id="what-is-glutathione">What glutathione actually is</h2>

      <p>
        Glutathione is a tripeptide — three amino acids, glutamate, cysteine and glycine, bonded
        together. Your liver makes it, every cell in your body uses it, and it is often called the
        <strong>master antioxidant</strong> because it does not just neutralise free radicals
        itself; it regenerates other antioxidants, including vitamins C and E, so they can keep
        working.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two things make it interesting for skin. First, cellular glutathione levels
        <strong>decline steadily with age</strong>, and that decline tracks with the oxidative
        damage that drives photoageing. Second — and this is the reason it dominates Indian
        pigmentation clinics — glutathione sits directly upstream of melanin production.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how-it-works">How it affects pigmentation</h2>

      <p>
        Melanin is produced by melanocytes through a pathway controlled by an enzyme called
        <strong>tyrosinase</strong>. Glutathione intervenes at two points in that pathway:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>It inhibits tyrosinase directly.</strong> Less enzyme activity means less melanin synthesis overall.</li>
        <li><strong>It shifts the type of melanin produced.</strong> Melanocytes make two pigments — eumelanin, which is dark brown-black, and pheomelanin, which is light red-yellow. Glutathione pushes the pathway toward pheomelanin.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        That second mechanism is the one people misunderstand. Glutathione does not bleach existing
        pigment. It changes what your melanocytes produce <em>going forward</em>, which is why the
        effect appears gradually and is strongest on areas of active, recent pigmentation — post-
        inflammatory marks, sun-triggered patches, uneven tone — rather than on your baseline
        complexion.
      </p>

