      <h2 id="evidence">What the oral trials found</h2>

      <p>
        The evidence base is smaller than for collagen, and it is not uniformly positive. The
        fairest one-line summary is that several small trials found a real effect, and the largest
        and most rigorous one did not.
      </p>

      <h3>Sarkar et al., 2025 — the current systematic review</h3>
      <p>
        The most recent attempt to pull this literature together ran in the
        <em>International Journal of Dermatology</em> (2025; 64(6):992–1004). Searching PubMed,
        Embase and Cochrane across the previous decade, the authors identified
        <strong>five randomised controlled trials and one open-label study in which oral
        glutathione significantly reduced the melanin index against placebo</strong> — at 250mg
        once daily, 250mg twice daily and 500mg once daily. That is the clearest statement
        currently available that the oral route does something measurable, and it is where the
        dose range quoted everywhere on this page comes from.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same review is blunt about the injectable route: it found
        <strong>a single placebo-controlled study of intravenous glutathione, and its result was
        not statistically significant.</strong>
      </p>

      <h3>Weschawalit et al., 2017 — 250mg daily, 12 weeks</h3>
      <p>
        A randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in middle-aged women compared reduced
        glutathione and oxidised glutathione against placebo. Both active groups showed
        <strong>reduced melanin index at multiple measurement sites</strong>, with additional
        improvements in skin elasticity and wrinkle depth. The dose — 250mg per day — is the one
        most reputable oral formulas are built around.
      </p>

      <h3>Arjinpathana &amp; Asawanonda, 2012 — 500mg daily, 4 weeks</h3>
      <p>
        A double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in Thai adults using 500mg daily found
        significantly lower melanin indices in the glutathione group compared with placebo after
        four weeks. It is a small, short study, but it established that oral dosing produces
        measurable change and not just self-reported impressions.
      </p>

      <h3>Richie et al., 2015 — the bioavailability question</h3>
      <p>
        This one is not a skin study, but it answers the most common objection. Six months of oral
        glutathione at 250mg and 1,000mg per day <strong>significantly increased body glutathione
        stores</strong> in blood, erythrocytes and buccal cells, in a dose-dependent way.
      </p>

      <h3>Sitohang et al., 2021 — the trial that missed</h3>
      <p>
        This is the result brands leave out, and it is the reason everything above is hedged. A
        randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial across three hospital clinics in
        Indonesia (<em>Journal of Clinical and Aesthetic Dermatology</em>, 2021;14(7):E53–E58)
        gave 83 adults aged 33 to 50 either a 500mg glutathione combination — with ascorbic acid,
        alpha-lipoic acid and zinc — or an identical placebo for 12 weeks. The supplement group
        improved more than placebo on spot UV, spot polarisation and skin tone.
        <strong>None of those differences reached statistical significance.</strong> The authors
        concluded the product was slightly beneficial in particular subgroups, which is a careful
        way of saying the trial did not show what it was designed to show.
      </p>

      <p>
        Be clear-eyed about the limitations overall: most of the positive studies are small, short
        and single-centre, several were run in Southeast Asian populations, and the effect sizes
        are modest even where they are significant — while the largest and longest of them came
        out neutral. Anyone presenting glutathione as a route to a dramatically different
        complexion is going far beyond what any of this supports. If you want the same treatment
        applied to safety rather than efficacy, we have written up
        <a href="/pages/collagen-side-effects">what the side-effect evidence actually records</a>.
      </p>

