      <h2 id="iv-glutathione">Oral vs injectable: the safety difference</h2>

      <p>
        This section matters more than the rest of the article. <strong>Intravenous glutathione for
        skin lightening is not an approved indication anywhere.</strong> It is an off-label
        cosmetic use of an injectable drug, and it has drawn formal safety advisories from
        regulators, including a well-publicised warning from the Philippine FDA following reports
        of serious adverse events.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>India now has its own notice.</strong> On 18 May 2026 the Central Drugs Standard
        Control Organisation issued a public notice to the effect that products supplied as
        injectable preparations do not fall within the legal definition of a cosmetic — which,
        under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940, covers articles applied by rubbing, pouring,
        sprinkling or spraying. Reporting on the notice describes it as aimed squarely at clinics
        and wellness centres selling glutathione "glow drips", skin-brightening shots and
        mesotherapy cocktails as cosmetic procedures. It is a clarification of existing law rather
        than a new ban, and it does not stop a qualified doctor using an approved drug under
        medical regulation. What it removes is the idea that a glutathione drip is a beauty
        treatment you can buy like a facial.
      </p>

      <p>Documented and reported risks of IV use for cosmetic lightening include:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Severe cutaneous adverse reactions, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome</li>
        <li>Kidney and liver dysfunction at high or repeated dosing</li>
        <li>Thyroid disruption</li>
        <li>Infection and embolism risk from unregulated administration in non-clinical settings</li>
        <li>Unpredictable product quality, since compounded cosmetic preparations are frequently unlicensed</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Oral glutathione at studied doses has a very different profile. It is generally well
        tolerated — in the Indonesian trial above, adverse events were mild and no more common
        than on placebo — it has been used in six-month trials without significant adverse
        effects, and its worst realistic outcome is that it does less than you hoped. That trade — a smaller,
        slower, safer effect — is the correct one for a cosmetic goal.
      </p>

